WHICH INSTRUCTION IS NEXT, AND HOW IS IT FOUND?

An English Major C architecture document.
Status: detailed experimental semantic draft; NOT verified machine-code execution.
Scope: ARMv7-A, T32 instruction state, unprivileged execution.
Reference: Arm DDI 0406C.d (normative architectural reference), with
Arm 100076_0200_00_en, sections A1.10, C1.3, C2.14, C2.19-C2.23,
C2.41, and C2.154 used as an additional readable assembly guide.
See "Instruction set architecture - sources and unfulfilled coverage.english".
This document elaborates "Given a machine - objects and execution.english".
It does NOT enlarge the set of permitted source operations.

PART ONE. THREE ADDRESSES THAT MUST NOT BE CONFUSED.

Define the CURRENT INSTRUCTION ADDRESS to be the virtual byte address
of the first halfword of the instruction whose effects are being specified.

Define the EXECUTION INDEX as follows, preserving the agreed definition:

Execution index: A value identifying the memory address from which the next
instruction is to be fetched. Ordinary sequential execution advances this
value according to the length of the instruction. Branching replaces it with
the address of another instruction.

The execution index is the address of an architectural instruction
boundary in the English Major C abstract execution model, not the
ordinal number of an instruction in a textual list. It is not a separate
CPU register. The architecture exposes R15, customarily called the
program counter; the English Major C term does not invent another
piece of hardware.

Define the PC-OPERAND VALUE of a T32 instruction at address A, for the
usual permitted PC reads, to be A plus four bytes. A PC-relative
instruction may further align this quantity according to its own
rule. ARM instruction descriptions, rather than this generic formula,
control special cases.

The PHYSICAL FETCH ADDRESS is not declared by this abstraction.
Actual instruction fetches can get ahead of execution, refill a cache,
fetch on a predicted path or discard speculatively fetched bytes.
Those events do not make the execution index an index into a pipeline.
No assumption about the number of stages, speculation policy, or
cache behavior is implied.

At address 0x00001002, the generic T32 PC operand equals 0x00001006.
If the instruction is two bytes long, its ordinary successor equals
0x00001004, which is NOT the PC-operand value.
If the instruction is four bytes long, its ordinary successor equals
0x00001006. Equality of those two numbers in this case does not make
their meanings identical.

PART TWO. HOW A T32 INSTRUCTION'S LENGTH IS IDENTIFIED.

T32 instructions occupy either one 16-bit halfword or two successive
16-bit halfwords, in instruction-stream order.

A 16-bit first halfword whose five highest bits are one of
11101, 11110, or 11111 marks a possible 32-bit T32 instruction; the
next halfword belongs to the same instruction. The high-five-bit
patterns other than those three mark a 16-bit T32 instruction.

This is a length distinction, not a proof that the 16-bit or 32-bit
encoding is valid. A decoded bit pattern may be allocated to an
instruction, reserved, undefined, unpredictable in its current
context, privileged, or dependent on a feature that is absent.
The decoder must consult the exact instruction tables.

The halfword is NOT automatically represented as an unsigned
application data value. It is an instruction-fetch unit, whose
bit fields are interpreted according to T32 instruction encoding
and the architecture's defined handling of instruction endianness.

If the first halfword signals a 32-bit T32 instruction, the second
halfword must be accounted for as part of that same instruction.
The semantic model must not advance two bytes after its first
halfword, treating the second halfword as an independent successor.

A T32 instruction address is divisible by two. A 32-bit T32
instruction may begin at an address congruent to two modulo four,
because its requirement is halfword alignment, not four-byte
instruction-address alignment.

PART THREE. ONE ARCHITECTURAL EXECUTION STEP.

Given the current execution index A and the complete relevant
architectural machine state:

First, determine that the mapping containing the instruction bytes
is permitted to be fetched and executed under the exact OS and
memory-management state.

Second, fetch enough instruction halfwords for T32 decoding.
Determine whether the encoded instruction is two or four bytes
long. The halfwords are one instruction when the first encoding
marks a 32-bit instruction.

Third, decode the instruction's operands, limitations, required
processor features, architectural condition and exact encoding
effects. Reject any attempted English Major C translation of
a form not permitted by the versioned operation grant, even
if the raw processor could execute that form.

Fourth, if the encoding is valid and the current conditional
execution state makes its effects conditional, determine the
architectural condition using the appropriate flags or operation
rule. A failed condition does not give permission to perform
a prohibited memory write, arithmetic effect or branch.
An instruction that is architecturally unconditionally executed
must not be given an invented conditional guard.

Fifth, if the instruction's condition passes, perform its specified
architectural effects, including reads, writes, flag changes,
control transfers, and possible exceptions. Whether an exception
occurs, and how it is delivered, are subject to the actual
architecture and environment, not a generic "skip on failure".

Sixth, determine the successor address, either by ordinary
sequential advancement or by the operation-specific control
transfer. Advance any active conditional-execution state in
accordance with the architecture. The exact ordering of condition
handling, ITSTATE advancement, PC writes and exceptions must be
validated against ARM DDI 0406C.d before executable semantics
are accepted. These English steps are an explanation of effects,
not a proposed processor pipeline timing diagram.

PART FOUR. A CONCRETE TWO-BYTE / FOUR-BYTE ADDRESS TRACE.

Assume that the following addresses contain executable,
architecturally valid T32 instructions, independently decoded
from actual bytes in a later experiment.

The instruction at address 0x00001000 occupies TWO bytes.
The instruction at address 0x00001002 occupies FOUR bytes.
The instruction at address 0x00001006 occupies TWO bytes.

If none transfers control, then the execution index successively
identifies 0x00001000, 0x00001002, 0x00001006, and 0x00001008.

The ordinary PC operand values while each of the first three
instructions executes are respectively 0x00001004, 0x00001006,
and 0x0000100A.

When the second instruction occupies address 0x1002 through the
end of the halfword at 0x1004, an instruction beginning at 0x1004
is NOT the ordinary sequential successor. An explicit branch
to 0x1004 must NOT be approved as a named, verified entry point
to the decoded instruction stream merely because 0x1004 is
halfword-aligned. A raw processor, if execution is permitted,
may decode those bytes differently starting at 0x1004.
This source-level prohibition is a checked-program boundary,
not a claim that every ARM processor automatically traps the
attempted mid-instruction branch.

This illustrative trace asserts address arithmetic only. It
does not assert the bytes, mnemonics, actual hardware behavior,
or timing of an executable whose bytes have not been inspected.

PART FIVE. HOW A DIRECT IMMEDIATE BRANCH REPLACES THE INDEX.

For a T32 B instruction beginning at address A, the branch
target is calculated from the ARM-defined PC-relative base
and the sign-extended displacement of its selected encoding,
subject to the encoding's range and alignment restrictions.

A conditional branch whose architectural condition is false
continues at A plus the instruction's length, without taking
the branch. It does not clear flags, perform an unrelated
memory write, or modify LR merely because the programmer
wanted to express an "if" statement.

An unconditional branch taken to legal target T makes T the
next execution index, regardless of the adjacent instruction
stream, while preserving all registers and flags except for
effects expressly specified by that instruction.
A B instruction does not write LR.

A programmer's label is a logical source name requiring an
actual resolved instruction address. The assembler or linker
cannot turn an out-of-range B encoding into an in-range one
by changing the architecture's immediate field; it may
instead produce an explicit veneer, whose additional actual
instructions must be translated and recorded separately.

PART SIX. HOW A CALL AND RETURN ARE REPRESENTED.

An immediate T32 BL is a branch that also writes LR. In
Thumb state, LR holds the address following the call with
the state indication bit set. A four-byte BL at address
0x00002002 normally leaves a Thumb return value of
0x00002007, because the next instruction begins at
0x00002006 and bit zero is used to indicate T32 return.

This low bit is metadata about the target instruction state,
not a request to execute from an odd byte boundary.
A return using BX LR reads LR's state indication:
bit zero equal to one requests T32; the actual address
has that low bit removed.
A value with bit zero equal to zero requests A32 and is
outside the present T32-only English Major C operation grant.

A BL does not save every register. It overwrites LR.
A nested call may therefore need a software convention for
preserving an earlier return address. Saving registers,
maintaining a stack, and obeying an ABI are separate
program requirements that cannot be inferred from BL's
single architectural effect.

A BLX through a register can choose A32 or T32 state from
the target register's low bit. In this restricted profile
only target values denoting T32 are permitted. The fact
that a physical ARMv7-A core supports interworking does
not mean that this first language grant permits arbitrary
execution in A32 state.

PART SEVEN. TWO DIFFERENT KINDS OF "NOT EXECUTING".

A conditional T32 instruction that has a false IT condition
belongs to the sequentially encountered instruction stream
but does not perform its normal conditional register or memory
effects. The following instruction address is still computed
using the current instruction's ACTUAL two-byte or four-byte
encoding length.

A conditional branch that evaluates false also follows the
sequential successor. The target of that untaken branch is
not the next architectural instruction.
These two situations may reach the same next address, but
have distinct encodings and possible machine-state effects.

A memory-protection fault, a malformed instruction encoding,
a disallowed instruction state, and an unsupported language
operation are NOT either of those cases. They require
different outcomes in the architecture or the verifier.

PART EIGHT. WHO DECIDES WHETHER MEMORY CONTENTS ARE EXECUTED.

A register can hold 0x00001006 as ordinary numerical data,
as a virtual address of another object, or as a value used
in computing the next instruction address. Only an
instruction that performs an architecturally permitted
control transfer can make that value become a next
instruction location in the execution trace.

A load of bytes from an executable mapping is not a call.
A store of bytes equal to a legal branch encoding is not
an executed branch. A table-branch load reads bytes as
offset data and uses the numerical value to calculate
a branch target; it does not execute the bytes of the table.

No instruction-cache-coherency operation or page-permission
change is authorized merely by declaring that stored bytes
will later be treated as machine instructions.

PART NINE. MISSING EVIDENCE AND TEST OBLIGATIONS.

Before claiming a concrete machine trace, attach the
exact executable bytes, their addresses, target state,
architecture variant, endian interpretation, permitted
instruction encoding and operands, register/flag snapshot,
memory map and execution permissions.

An independent reviewer must check:
  1. one valid two-byte instruction's successor;
  2. one valid four-byte instruction's successor;
  3. a four-byte instruction at an address equal to two mod four;
  4. PC operand reads versus sequential successor for each width;
  5. a conditional branch taken and not taken on different flags;
  6. a BL followed by BX LR without silently removing bit zero;
  7. an illegal A32 target rejected by the T32-only grant;
  8. an attempted branch into an unverified second halfword rejected
     by the checked English representation;
  9. an instruction-fetch permission failure distinguished from
     a harmless data read;
 10. an unsupported instruction rejected rather than translated
     into a similar, but different, permitted operation.

No listed case is marked as already executed. The current
document is source-level reasoning, subject to the cited
reference manual and future independent acceptance.
